---
import Subpage from "../layouts/Subpage.astro";
import { SITE } from "../data/site";
---

<Subpage
  title="Privacy: nothing you type leaves your computer"
  description="Nothing you type leaves your computer. What unflub's anonymous daily usage stats contain, with no ID attached, and how to turn them off in Settings."
  crumb="Privacy"
>
  <h1>Nothing you type leaves your computer.</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Corrections happen on your computer. The code is public and free for anyone to read. Here is exactly what unflub shares, and
    how to turn it off.
  </p>

  <h2>What you type</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Corrections are worked out on your computer. Your text is never sent anywhere.</li>
    <li>
      unflub holds the word you are typing, and the one before it for context, in memory. It never writes what you type to disk.
    </li>
    <li>The words you misspell, and what they were fixed to, are never collected.</li>
    <li>
      Password fields are always skipped. Terminals and password managers are skipped by default, and you can add any other app.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Usage stats are on by default</h2>
  <p>
    unflub is free and self-funded. Once a day it sends a short, anonymous usage report. It helps us see what breaks, which
    features people use and where autocorrect gets it wrong.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Usage stats are on when you install unflub. The first-run screen says: "unflub is free and self-funded. By continuing, you
      agree to the <a href="/terms/">Terms</a>."
    </li>
    <li>
      You can turn them off at any moment in Settings &gt; Privacy. It takes effect right away, and nothing more is sent after
      that. Counts not yet sent are deleted.
    </li>
    <li>There is one report a day, and every count in it covers that day only.</li>
    <li>Reports carry no ID, so nothing ties one report to the next or to you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the daily report contains</h2>
  <p>
    Every value is a count, a yes or no, or a choice from a short fixed list. Our server refuses a report with anything else in
    it.
  </p>

  <h3>The app and your computer</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>the unflub version, its release channel (stable or beta) and your processor type, x64 or ARM</li>
    <li>
      your Windows version, in broad groups: older than Windows 10 22H2, Windows 10 22H2, Windows 11 22H2, 23H2, 24H2, 25H2, or newer
    </li>
    <li>how you installed unflub: the installer, winget, Chocolatey, Scoop, the Microsoft Store or a portable copy</li>
    <li>how long ago you installed it: today, 1 to 6 days, 7 to 29 days, 30 to 89 days, or 90 days or more</li>
    <li>
      the language of your keyboard layout: English, French, Spanish, German, Portuguese, Italian, Dutch or other
    </li>
    <li>the language unflub's settings window is set to</li>
    <li>processor cores and memory, in ranges such as 5 to 8 cores or 9 to 16 GB</li>
    <li>whether you finished, skipped or have not started the welcome tour</li>
    <li>whether this is the first report this week and this month, so we can count weekly and monthly users</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Your settings</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>the engine mode, the pill style, the theme, and light or dark</li>
    <li>how many apps you left out, how many personal words and how many custom rules you have, never the apps or words</li>
    <li>whether automatic updates are on</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How corrections went</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>how many words unflub fixed, and how many fixes the pill showed</li>
    <li>how many fixes you started to undo with Backspace, and how many you undid</li>
    <li>what ended a fixed word: a space, punctuation, or Enter or Tab</li>
    <li>where each fix came from: unflub's own dictionary, Windows spell check or one of your rules</li>
    <li>how many fixes unflub stopped making after repeated undos, and how many words you added to your list</li>
    <li>minutes autocorrect was on while you typed, times you turned it off, and minutes it was paused</li>
    <li>times unflub left a word alone because it was in a password field, an app you left out, or code</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What kind of app</h3>
  <p>
    Fixes per kind of app: browser, chat, code editor, office, notes, email or other. Your computer works out the kind from a
    list built into unflub, and the app's name never leaves your computer. Terminals are never corrected, so they have no group.
  </p>

  <h3>Features you use</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      how often you open Settings, open the Stats page, replay the tour, use a keyboard shortcut and change the theme
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Updates</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>how many updates were offered, downloaded, installed, failed or dismissed</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Speed and health</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      how long unflub takes to decide on a fix and to type it, and how long a Windows spell check takes, as the typical and slowest
      times with how many were measured
    </li>
    <li>how much memory unflub uses while idle</li>
    <li>how many times unflub crashed</li>
    <li>
      how many times unflub set up its keyboard hook again and why (a check found it missing, the computer woke from sleep, or it
      was unlocked), and how many times the hook could not start
    </li>
    <li>how many times unflub paused itself because another program got in the way of keyboard input</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is never collected</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>what you type, any word, or the words you misspell and what they were fixed to</li>
    <li>window titles, app or program names, or file paths</li>
    <li>your Windows user name or your computer's name</li>
    <li>your IP address: our server needs it to answer, like any web request, and never stores it</li>
    <li>an ID for your copy of unflub or your computer, or anything else that links one report to another</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Crash reports</h2>
  <p>
    If unflub crashes, nothing is sent then. The next time it starts, Settings asks whether to send the crash report: Send, Don't
    send or Always send. A report holds where in the code it failed and technical details such as the unflub and Windows
    versions. Text you typed is never in it, and your user folder is cut out of every file path.
  </p>

  <h2>The daily update check</h2>
  <p>
    Once a day, unflub downloads a signed list of releases from our server. Everyone gets the same file, and unflub decides on
    your computer whether an update applies. The request carries only the unflub version, such as {SITE.version}. If an update
    applies, unflub downloads it, checks its signature and installs it when you restart or quit unflub, never while you type.
    You can turn off Automatic updates in Settings &gt; Privacy.
  </p>

  <h2>Your Stats page stays home</h2>
  <p>
    The Stats page in the app shows your corrections this week, the words you get wrong most and how many keystrokes unflub
    saved you. It is worked out and kept on your computer, and none of it is sent.
  </p>

  <h2>No account, no ads</h2>
  <p>There is no account and no advertising ID. We don't sell or share data, and unflub shows no ads.</p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The site is hosted on Vercel and doesn't set cookies. We count visits with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which uses no cookies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Downloads are verified by Cloudflare Turnstile. When you point at or tab to a download button, your browser loads
    Turnstile from Cloudflare, and clicking runs a quick check before the download starts. What Cloudflare collects for that
    check is covered by its <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">Turnstile Privacy Addendum</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you arrived from a link with a source on it, such as <span class="path">?ref=hn</span>, this tab remembers that source
    until you close it, so the download is counted under it.
  </p>

  <h2>Your settings</h2>
  <p>
    Your settings, your words and your shortcuts are saved in a file on your computer. Uninstalling unflub removes the app and its
    login entry, and the uninstaller asks whether to delete that file too.
  </p>

  <h2>Check for yourself</h2>
  <p>
    The code is public, so you do not have to take our word for any of this. <a href={SITE.repo}>Read it on GitHub</a>,
    or <a href={SITE.issues}>open an issue</a> if you have a question. The <a href="/terms/">terms of use</a> cover the rest.
  </p>

  <p class="small updated">
    Last updated <time datetime="2026-11-25">November 25, 2026</time>. unflub is an <a href={SITE.org.url}>RMGN</a> product.
  </p>
</Subpage>

